<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Binding of Isaac</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Akedah, Sacrifice of Isaac</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Binding of Isaac is the story in Genesis 22 in which, according to the text, God orders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> to offer his son Isaac as a sacrifice on a mountain in the region of Moriah. After Abraham has bound Isaac to an altar, an angel stops him and a ram is sacrificed instead. Jewish tradition interprets it in many ways, among them as a test of Abraham's faith, and some Christian writers saw Isaac as foreshadowing Christ. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> the son is unnamed; early scholars disagreed whether he was Isaac or Ishmael, and Muslims commemorate the submission of Abraham and his son at Eid al-Adha. Some scholars suggest an older version in which Isaac was killed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac.jpg" alt="Binding of Isaac" width="330" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caravaggio / Bartolomeo Cavarozzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacrifice_of_Isaac-Caravaggio_(c._1603).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samaritans hold that the <b>Binding of Isaac</b> took place on Mount Gerizim, not at Jerusalem's Temple Mount, and in their version of the Ten Commandments the tenth commands an altar to be built on Gerizim.</blockquote><a href="/religion/samaritanism">Samaritanism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
